      {
         "id": "SECTION_japan",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### In Asia (Japan): The Soroban\n\nDuring the 16th century, according to the Gregorian calendar, the *suanpan* (算盤) reached Japan, where it became known as the *soroban* (算盤). This soroban is the closest approximation to the Roman *calculi* we saw earlier in this class (<htcite0>).\n\nWrite the proposed number on the soroban by moving the beads.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "339c7ff5-8365-49bb-a1cd-ad5a13d5fd86",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Building to count (Soroban, 算盤)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"atwImgAsia3\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('atwImgAsia3', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 9</b>: Asia on the world map. Map from Mapswire (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "https://mapswire.com",
